  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="1">Tracking: read 082's status in the word-add-in-r1 worktree, #1046, and PR #1045's state; message the word-add-in-r1 session with this task's plan. Re-measure the live role (privileges, depths, holders) — do not inherit the 2026-09-30 list. Record both in the task note. If 082 has not started or is stalled, fire the first escalation trigger now — do not wait.</step>
    <step order="2">Enumerate the tables any writer can assign to the secure team: Office I-6 writers (082's list), the #1034 writers, and the C10 part-2 scope (tasks 146, 147, 148 POMLs; design.md §5.1d; the matter and work-assignment child sets from live `OneToManyRelationships`). Record writer file:line (or task id) per table.</step>
    <step order="3">Once 082 has landed: verify its five-table grant live (depth, holder) and that the guide rows read the codified file. For each ADDITIONAL table from step 2: negative control first — a probe re-own of a row of that table to the secure owner team is refused and the refusal names the missing privilege (record it). Remember the principal-privilege cache lags (guide §7: stable across 3 polls, cross-check privilegeCount).</step>
    <step order="4">Extend 082's JSON file with the additional tables (reason per entry); run 082's script dry run and record the plan; apply; re-run each probe until stable; read back `owningteam`; delete the probes.</step>
    <step order="5">After task 144: add the census clause (in `SecureBuRoleDepthAssertion`, reading the same JSON file): the role lacks the required privilege on any table in the set → FAIL naming the table; the role is held by anything but the named team → FAIL (clause 3, as 144 retargets it). Seed both ways in unit tests and run it by hand against dev; record both outputs.</step>
    <step order="6">New environments: add the setup (Secure BU, named owner team per task 144, role created in the Secure BU, privileges from the codified file via the script, role assigned to the named team, default team holding no role) to the provisioning pipeline — a control-plane handler step or an operator-runbook step in SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md §12 — per the owner's answer under the pipeline escalation trigger. Task 150's FLS setup joins the same step (whichever task lands first creates it; the other extends it).</step>
    <step order="7">Drift: record the owner's answer from 082; if 082 did not ask, put the drift decision to the owner as a 🔔 with a recommendation and record the answer.</step>
    <step order="8">Update TASK-INDEX.md (`✅ [done]`) and the POML status; close #1046 once, coordinating with word-add-in-r1 so it cites both 082 and this task; write the task note.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>OPEN OWNER QUESTION — who applies the five-table grant. The owner told UAC-r2 to actively track this gap; word-add-in-r1 then recorded ownership of the same change in its task 082 "at the owner's instruction". If 082 has not landed by the time step 3 is due, or is stalled, STOP and ask the owner. Recommendation: keep 082 as the implementer (the agreed split, and PR #1045's Office To Dos depend on it) with this task tracking it to closure; if the owner wants it done now, this task applies 082's scope under 082's own contract (JSON data file, script, guide rows) with the peer's agreement, so nothing is done twice.</trigger>
    <trigger>Word-add-in-r1 task 082 is in progress or plans a set that conflicts with step 2's table list when this task starts: STOP before any live write and reconcile with the peer (and the owner if they disagree); do not race the peer on a shared role.</trigger>
    <trigger>A probe re-own is refused naming anything other than `Read` (e.g. AppendTo on the parent, Assign): record the exact message and escalate before granting — the guide's evidence says Read alone suffices, and a different answer changes the least-privilege design.</trigger>
    <trigger>Removing or narrowing ANY existing privilege on this role (the drift) — owner decision under CLAUDE.md §6.</trigger>
    <trigger>OPEN OWNER QUESTION — pipeline route. Adding the setup to the control-plane pipeline requires a new handler id or changes the handler DAG owned by customer-provisioning-orchestration; the alternative is an operator-runbook step in SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md §12. Recommendation: a control-plane handler step (a runbook step is the hand-edited path that produced today's drift), coordinated with customer-provisioning-orchestration — surface the design to the owner before building it.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>The only procedure that sets this role is hand-run PowerShell in `SECURE-PROJECT-ENVIRONMENT-SETUP.md` §5.3-§5.4 (`:217-257`). Grep of `src/server/services` for `Secure Record` returns nothing; grep of `SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md` for `Secure Record` returns nothing. Word-add-in-r1 task 082 creates the JSON codified set and `scripts/Set-SecureRecordOwnerRolePrivileges.ps1` (not yet on this branch). `SecureBuRoleDepthAssertion.cs:67` pins the role name; its clauses (2 at `:240-258`, 3 after it) check holders, not table coverage.</existing>
    <extension>Everything here extends something existing: 082's JSON file (entries added), 082's script (reused), the NFR-05 census (one clause added), and the customer-provisioning runbook/handlers (one step). No new file of this task's own except its note; no second list anywhere.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Every secure-record To Do is refused by Dataverse today; every environment built from the guide also refuses secure-target document saves; re-running guide §5.4 in dev removes prvReadsprk_Document and breaks secure saves there with no signal; task 146's re-own of any table outside 082's five (invoice, analysis, communicationthread, agreement, …) is refused, so those children stay in ordinary BUs; a new customer environment has no Secure BU role at all.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

